title:    The endpoint address is ours, and it is not a Google VIP
subtitle: Reserved in this VPC, and deliberately outside every subnet range
width:    980
---
$ gcloud compute addresses describe psc-apis-endpoint-ip --global
  10.99.0.2
  PRIVATE_SERVICE_CONNECT
  INTERNAL

$ gcloud compute networks subnets list --filter="network~psc-lab"
  psc-lab-us-central1	10.40.0.0/24	True

$ gcloud compute forwarding-rules describe pscapis --global
  pscapis	all-apis	10.99.0.2

# 10.99.0.2 is not inside 10.40.0.0/24, and that is required, not incidental:
# a PSC endpoint "can't be within the range of subnets configured in the VPC
# network". Private Google Access, by contrast, sends traffic to 199.36.153.8/30
# - addresses Google owns, reached through the default internet gateway.
#
# The True on the subnet line is Private Google Access. Both mechanisms are
# live on this one VM at the same time, which is what makes the next card a
# controlled comparison rather than two separate experiments.
